Q: What is Inversion of Control (IoC) Container in Spring?

Old way: you create objects yourself (`new Service()`, `new Repo()`).
IoC: you **invert that control** — Spring creates objects, wires dependencies, manages lifecycle.

Primary way IoC is implemented in Spring = **Dependency Injection (DI)**.

────────────────────────────────────────
🧠 Simple idea

Without IoC: chef does everything personally.
With IoC: you place order; kitchen (container) prepares and serves.

Developer → defines what is needed  
Container → creates beans + injects dependencies

────────────────────────────────────────
🔧 What the IoC container does

1) Creates objects (beans)
2) Injects dependencies
3) Manages lifecycle (init → use → destroy)
4) Applies scopes (singleton/prototype/…)

In Spring Boot, container starts with `SpringApplication.run(...)`.

────────────────────────────────────────
Types of IoC container

1️⃣ BeanFactory (basic)
- Interface: `org.springframework.beans.factory.BeanFactory`
- Lightweight, typically lazy getBean
- Rarely used directly today

2️⃣ ApplicationContext (advanced — preferred)
- Interface: `org.springframework.context.ApplicationContext`
- Extends BeanFactory
- Eagerly creates singletons by default
- Events, i18n, AOP integration, web support

| Feature             | BeanFactory | ApplicationContext      |
| ------------------- | ----------- | ----------------------- |
| Basic IoC           | Yes         | Yes                     |
| Singleton startup   | Lazy-ish    | Eager by default        |
| Events / i18n / web | No/limited  | Yes                     |
| Modern apps / Boot  | ❌ direct   | ✅                      |

────────────────────────────────────────
How IoC works in Spring Boot

1) DI styles
- Constructor injection ✅ preferred
- Setter injection
- Field `@Autowired` (works; less test-friendly)

2) Component stereotypes become beans
`@Component`, `@Service`, `@Repository`, `@Controller` / `@RestController`

3) Explicit beans
`@Configuration` + `@Bean`

4) Component scanning
Scans main class package + subpackages (`@SpringBootApplication` includes `@ComponentScan`).

Example contrast:
// old
UserService service = new UserService(new UserRepository());

// Spring IoC
@Service
class UserService {
    private final UserRepository repo;
    public UserService(UserRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }
}

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview one-liner

“IoC means Spring controls object creation and wiring instead of us using new. The IoC container (ApplicationContext in Boot) creates beans, injects dependencies, and manages their lifecycle — mainly through Dependency Injection.”
